<div class=3D"note note--neutral" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <!-- When t=
here's no title, emoji is above content -->
    <div class=3D"note__layout" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D"">  <div class=
=3D"note__body" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <div class=3D"note__content" =
data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <code>acc_pmi</code> metric measures how much=
 more likely a model is to predict A_i if provided with question context co=
mpared to if there was no context at all. This can be useful if the correct=
 choice contains generally unlikely tokens, making the model less likely to=
 choose such an answer. </div> </div> </div> </div>=20
<p>For our generative tasks on the other hand, we used the following metric=
s:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>prefix_match</code>: Exact match where only the prefix of the ans=
wer must match</li>
<li><code>f1</code>: F1 score computed over predicted/gold words extracted =
using a word tokenizer</li>
</ul>
<p>For both generative metrics, minor preprocessing is applied to remove ar=
ticles and punctuation, and lowercase the text.</p>
<p>Selecting the best evaluation metrics proved to be a <strong>challenging=
 task</strong>. Not only is there no single metric that consistently outper=
forms the rest, but we often encountered situations where one metric had be=
tter monotonicity while another had a higher signal-to-noise ratio. In such=
 cases, we typically made our decision based on the selected metric for tas=
ks=E2=80=99 implementation in a different language. We are aware that such =
hand-picking is often not possible and thus offer the following recommendat=
ions:</p>
<p>=E2=9E=A1=EF=B8=8F Multichoice Tasks</p>
<ul>
<li>We found <strong>base accuracy</strong> to perform well for tasks with =
answer options varying subtly (e.g. Yes/No/Also), particularly NLI tasks. I=
n such cases, where the answer options are often each a single token, the b=
ase accuracy is advisable to use.</li>
<li>While OLMES authors (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.08446" targe=
t=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">Gu et al., 2024</a>) recommends us=
ing PMI for tasks with unusual words, we found <strong>PMI</strong> to be h=
ighly effective for =E2=80=9Cdifficult=E2=80=9D reasoning and knowledge tas=
ks like AGIEVAL or MMLU. In these cases, PMI provided the best results and =
was often the only metric delivering performance above random. That said, P=
MI was, on average, the weakest metric across all other tasks, while also b=
eing two times more expensive to compute. We therefore only recommend its u=
se for complex reasoning and knowledge tasks.</li>
<li>The metrics we found to be <strong>most reliable overall</strong> were =
length normalization metrics (token or character-based). However, the best =
choice was dependent on language, rather than being consistent for a given =
task. Due to that, we recommend using the maximum of acc_char and acc_token=
 for the most reliable results.<d-footnote>Note that acc_token is heavily t=
okenizer dependent. On our ablations all models were trained using the same=
 tokenizer.</d-footnote></li>
</ul>
<p>=E2=9E=A1=EF=B8=8F Generative Tasks</p>
<p>For <strong>generative metrics</strong>, the choice is clearer: we sugge=
st using the F1 score unless exact matching is required, as in math-related=
 tasks. F1 is generally less noisy and more resilient to small changes in t=
he generations.</p>
<h2 id=3D"creating-your-own-evaluation"><a href=3D"https://openevals-evalua=
tion-guidebook.hf.space/#creating-your-own-evaluation">Creating your own ev=
aluation</a></h2>
<p>At this stage, you likely have a good idea of why people do evaluation, =
which benchmarks exist and are relevant for different model stages (trainin=
g, inference of base and tuned models), but what if nothing exists for your=
 specific use case?</p>
<p>This is precisely when you could want to create your own evaluation.</p>
<h3 id=3D"dataset"><a href=3D"https://openevals-evaluation-guidebook.hf.spa=
ce/#dataset">Dataset</a></h3>
<h4 id=3D"using-existing-data"><a href=3D"https://openevals-evaluation-guid=
ebook.hf.space/#using-existing-data">Using existing data</a></h4>
<p>You can use existing datasets are are, and change the prompting or metri=
cs associated (as has been done for older evaluations to adapt them to new =
prompting method), but you can also aggregate datasets.</p>
<p>Dataset aggregation is a good approach when you want to evaluate a speci=
fic capability that isn=E2=80=99t well-covered by a single benchmark. Rathe=
r than starting from scratch, you can combine samples from multiple existin=
g datasets to create a targeted evaluation suite. That=E2=80=99s for exampl=
es what the authors of the =E2=80=9CMeasuring AGI=E2=80=9D paper did recent=
ly to try to create a new =E2=80=9CAGI evaluation=E2=80=9D dataset.</p>
<p>When aggregating datasets, pay attention to whether</p>
<div class=3D"sidenote-wrapper" style=3D"position: relative; display: block=
;"><ul>
<li>they contain redundant data (most mathematics datasets are rewrites or =
aggregations of the same initial problems)</li>
<li>you need balanced representation across sources (you might not want one=
 dataset to dominate and skew your evaluation) - this will also determine w=
hether to aggregate scores across all samples or per subset</li>
<li>formats and difficulty levels are compatible (typically, if creating a =
unified dataset, beware of mixing up samples requiring sampling or not).</l=
i>
</ul><div class=3D"sidenote-container" style=3D"position: absolute; top: 0p=
x; right: -292px; width: 260px; display: block; opacity: 1;"> <aside class=
=3D"sidenote"> Examples: MMLU, Big-Bench (hundreds of diverse tasks), and H=
ELM (combines multiple existing benchmarks for holistic evaluation) </aside=
> </div></div>
 =20
<p>New research by EpochAI (2025) showcases how to <a href=3D"https://epoch=
.ai/blog/a-rosetta-stone-for-ai-benchmarks" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopen=
er noreferrer">best aggregate benchmarks together under a single framework<=
/a> to make the aggregated dataset harder overall and less prone to saturat=
ion.</p>
<h4 id=3D"using-human-annotators"><a href=3D"https://openevals-evaluation-g=
uidebook.hf.space/#using-human-annotators">Using human annotators</a></h4>
<p>I suggest reading Section 3 of this <a href=3D"https://aclanthology.org/=
2024.cl-3.1/" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">review</a> of g=
ood practices in data annotation quality. If you want production level qual=
ity and have the means to implement all of these methods, go ahead!</p>
