<p>Once you have an accuracy score per sample, you can <strong>aggregate</s=
trong> it across your whole set in several ways. In general, people average=
 their results, but you can do more complex things depending on your needs.=
 (Some metrics already come with an aggregation, like CorpusBLEU).</p>
<div class=3D"sidenote-wrapper" style=3D"position: relative; display: block=
;"><p>If your score is <strong>binary</strong>, look at the <strong>precisi=
on</strong> (critical when false positives are costly), <strong>recall</str=
ong> (critical when missing positives is costly), <strong>F1 score</strong>=
 (balances precision and recall, good for imbalanced data), or <strong>MCC<=
/strong> (Matthews Correlation Coefficient, which works well with imbalance=
d datasets by considering all confusion matrix elements).
If your score is <strong>continuous</strong> (less likely though), you can =
use <strong>mean squared error</strong> (penalizes large errors but heavily=
 weights outliers) or <strong>mean absolute error</strong> (more balanced t=
han MSE). </p><div class=3D"sidenote-container" style=3D"position: absolute=
; top: 0px; right: -292px; width: 260px; display: block; opacity: 1;"> <asi=
de class=3D"sidenote">  If you assume your data should follow a specific li=
near regression model (for example if you are studying model calibration), =
you can look at measures like the <strong>R=C2=B2</strong> or correlation c=
oefficients like <strong>Pearson</strong> (for linear relationships, assume=
s normality) or <strong>Spearman</strong> (for monotonic relationships with=
out normality assumptions). However, it=E2=80=99s a bit out of scope here. =
 </aside> </div></div>  <p></p>
<p>More generally, when picking your metric and its aggregation, you need t=
o keep in mind what your task is really about. For some domains (ex: medica=
l, chatbots with public interaction), you don=E2=80=99t want to measure the=
 average performance, but need a way to evaluate the <strong>worst performa=
nce</strong> you=E2=80=99ll get (on medical quality of output, on toxicity,=
 etc).</p>
<div class=3D"note note--neutral" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <!-- When t=
here's a title, emoji is inline with title -->
    <div class=3D"note__body" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <div class=3D"n=
ote__header" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D"">  <div class=3D"note__title" data=
-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D"">To go further</div> </div> <div class=3D"note__con=
tent" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <ul>
<li>This <a href=3D"https://ehudreiter.com/2024/07/10/challenges-in-evaluat=
ing-llms/" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">blog</a> covers so=
me of the challenges of evaluating LLMs.</li>
<li>If you=E2=80=99re looking for metrics, you=E2=80=99ll also find a good =
list with description, score ranges and use cases in <a href=3D"https://hug=
gingface.co/evaluate-metric" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">=
this organisation</a>.</li>
</ul> </div> </div> </div>=20
<div class=3D"note note--neutral" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <!-- When t=
here's a title, emoji is inline with title -->
    <div class=3D"note__body" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <div class=3D"n=
ote__header" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D"">  <div class=3D"note__title" data=
-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D"">Pros and cons of using automated metrics</div> </d=
iv> <div class=3D"note__content" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <p>Automated=
 benchmarks have the following advantages:</p><ul>
<li><strong>Consistency and reproducibility</strong>: You can run the same =
automated benchmark 10 times on the same model and you=E2=80=99ll get the s=
ame results (baring variations in hardware or inherent model randomness). T=
his means that you can easily create fair rankings of models for a given ta=
sk.</li>
<li><strong>Scale at limited cost</strong>: They are one of the cheapest wa=
y to evaluate models at the moment.</li>
<li><strong>Understandability</strong>: Most automated metrics are very und=
erstandable.</li>
</ul><p>However, they also present have a <strong>reduced use on more compl=
ex tasks</strong>: an automatic metric either requires you to have a perfec=
t, unique and unambiguous reference/gold, like for tasks where performance =
is easy to define and assess (for example, classification of toxicity, know=
ledge questions with a single answer). More complex capabilities, on the ot=
her hand, are harder to decompose into a single and simple answer.</p> </di=
v> </div> </div>=20
<h4 id=3D"normalization"><a href=3D"https://openevals-evaluation-guidebook.=
hf.space/#normalization">Normalization</a></h4>
<p>Normalization means changing a string of characters to have it fit a spe=
cific reference format. For example, when comparing a model prediction to a=
 reference, you usually don=E2=80=99t want to penalize extra spacing in the=
 prediction, or added punctuation or capitalisation. That=E2=80=99s why you=
 normalize your prediction.</p>
<p>They are vital for specific tasks, such as math evaluations, where you w=
ant to extract an equation from a longer prediction, and compare it to a re=
ference.
In the below table, we make a list of some issues we saw happening when ext=
racting predictions from model outputs using SymPy naively for the MATH dat=
aset, and how Math-Verify, a specific math parser, solved these.</p>



























































