<li>
<p><strong>maj@n</strong> (majority voting): Sample n generations and take =
the most frequent answer. This helps filter out spurious outputs and works =
particularly well when the model=E2=80=99s correct reasoning path is more c=
onsistent than its errors. Commonly used for math and reasoning tasks.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>cot@n</strong> (chain-of-thought sampling): Sample n reasoning t=
races and evaluate them. Can be combined with majority voting or a pass@k (=
sample n reasoning chains, extract final answers, take majority or a thresh=
old).</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>avg@n</strong> (stable average score): Average the scores across=
 n samples. It=E2=80=99s a more stable estimator of performance than using =
=E2=80=9Cbest=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Cmost common=E2=80=9D case.</p>
</li>
</ul>
