<p>When you use sampling evaluations, make sure to always report all sampli=
ng parameters (temperature, top-p, k value) as they significantly affect re=
sults.</p>
<div class=3D"note note--neutral" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <!-- When t=
here's a title, emoji is inline with title -->
    <div class=3D"note__body" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <div class=3D"n=
ote__header" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D"">  <div class=3D"note__title" data=
-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D"">When can you use sampling and when shouldn't you?<=
/div> </div> <div class=3D"note__content" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <ul=
>
<li><strong>For training evaluation/ablations</strong>: =E2=9D=8C Generally=
 avoid sampling metrics as they=E2=80=99re expensive and add variance. Stic=
k to greedy decoding with a fixed seed.</li>
<li><strong>For post-training evaluation</strong>: =E2=9C=85 Sampling metri=
cs can reveal capabilities that greedy decoding misses (especially for more=
 complex tasks requiring reasoning, math or code).</li>
<li><strong>At inference</strong>: =E2=9C=85 These metrics help estimate ho=
w much improvement you can get from sampling multiple times at inference. I=
t=E2=80=99s particularly cool when you want to study how far you can push s=
mall models with test time compute.</li>
</ul><p>However, keep in mind that sampling k times multiplies your evaluat=
ion cost by k. For expensive models or large datasets, this adds up very qu=
ickly!</p> </div> </div> </div>=20
<h4 id=3D"functional-scorers"><a href=3D"https://openevals-evaluation-guide=
book.hf.space/#functional-scorers">Functional scorers</a></h4>
<p>Instead of comparing generated text to a reference through fuzzy string =
matching, functional testing evaluates whether outputs satisfy specific ver=
ifiable constraints. This approach is extremely promising because it=E2=80=
=99s more flexible and allows =E2=80=9Cinfinite=E2=80=9D updates of the tes=
t case through rule-based generation (which reduces overfitting).</p>
<p><strong>IFEval and IFBench</strong> are excellent examples of this appro=
ach for instruction following evaluation. Rather than asking =E2=80=9Cdoes =
this text match a reference answer?=E2=80=9D, they ask =E2=80=9Cdoes this t=
ext satisfy formatting constraints given in the instructions?=E2=80=9D</p>
<p>For instance, instructions might specify:</p>
<ul>
<li><em>=E2=80=9CInclude exactly 3 bullet points=E2=80=9D</em> =E2=86=92 ve=
rify the output contains exactly 3 bullets</li>
<li><em>=E2=80=9CCapitalize only the first sentence=E2=80=9D</em> =E2=86=92=
 parse and check capitalization patterns</li>
<li><em>=E2=80=9CUse the word =E2=80=98algorithm=E2=80=99 at least twice=E2=
=80=9D</em> =E2=86=92 count word occurrences</li>
<li><em>=E2=80=9CYour response must be in JSON format with keys =E2=80=98an=
swer=E2=80=99 and =E2=80=98reasoning=E2=80=99=E2=80=9D</em> =E2=86=92 valid=
ate JSON structure</li>
</ul>
<p>Each constraint can be checked with a specific rule-based verifier, maki=
ng these evaluations more unambiguous, interpretable, fast, and considerabl=
y less costly than using models as judges.</p>
<div class=3D"sidenote-wrapper" style=3D"position: relative; display: block=
;"><p>This functional approach works particularly well for instruction foll=
owing, but requires creativity to extend to other text properties. The key =
is identifying aspects of text that can be verified programmatically rather=
 than through semantic comparison.</p><div class=3D"sidenote-container" sty=
le=3D"position: absolute; top: 0px; right: -292px; width: 260px; display: b=
lock; opacity: 1;"> <aside class=3D"sidenote"> <p>Functional testing is ins=
pired by code evaluation, where functional testing through unit tests is st=
andard practice (checking if generated code produces correct outputs for gi=
ven inputs).</p> </aside> </div></div>
 =20
<h3 id=3D"with-humans"><a href=3D"https://openevals-evaluation-guidebook.hf=
.space/#with-humans">With humans</a></h3>
<p>Human evaluation is simply asking humans to score predictions.</p>
<div class=3D"sidenote-wrapper" style=3D"position: relative; display: block=
;"><p>Human evaluation is very interesting, because of its <strong>flexibil=
ity</strong> (if you define clearly enough what you are evaluating, you can=
 get scores for about anything!), <strong>inherent un-contamination</strong=
> (if humans write new questions to test your system, they should not be pr=
esent in your training data, hopefully), and <strong>good correlation with =
human preference</strong> for obvious reasons.</p><div class=3D"sidenote-co=
ntainer" style=3D"position: absolute; top: 0px; right: -292px; width: 260px=
; display: block; opacity: 1;"> <aside class=3D"sidenote"> <p>However, when=
 doing evaluation with humans, you need to make sure your annotators are di=
verse enough that your results generalizes.</p> </aside> </div></div>
 =20
<p>Different approaches exist to evaluate models with humans in the loop.</=
p>
<p><strong>Vibe-checks</strong> is the name given to manual evaluations don=
e by individual members of the community, usually on undisclosed prompts, t=
o get an overall =E2=80=9Cfeeling=E2=80=9D of how well models perform on th=
eir use cases of preference. (I=E2=80=99ve also seen the term =E2=80=9Ccana=
ry-testing=E2=80=9D used for this, in reference to high signal canary in a =
coalmine approach). Said use cases can be anything from the most exciting t=
o the most mundate - to cite some I=E2=80=99ve seen on Reddit, they covered=
 legal questions in German, coding, tool use, quality of erotica written, e=
tc. Often shared on forums or social media, they mostly constitute anecdota=
l evidence, and tend to be highly sensitive to confirmation bias (in other =
words, people tend to find what they look for).</p>
