</div></div></figure>
<p>This section is therefore a bit long, because you need to be well aware =
of the limitations of using model as judges: a lot of people are blindly ju=
mping into using them because they seem easier than actually working with h=
umans or designing new metrics, but then end up with uninsterpretable data =
with tricky bias to extract.</p>
<p>My main personal gripe with using models as judges is that they introduc=
e very subtle and un-interpretable bias in the answer selection. I feel tha=
t, much like when crossbreeding too much in genetics studies, you end up wi=
th dysfunctional animals or plants, by using LLMs to select and train LLMs,=
 we are just as likely to introduce minute changes that will have bigger re=
percussions a couple generations down the line. I believe this type of bias=
 is less likely to occur in smaller and more specialized models as judges (=
such as toxicity classifiers), but this remains to be rigorously tested and=
 proven.</p>
<div class=3D"note note--neutral" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <!-- When t=
here's a title, emoji is inline with title -->
    <div class=3D"note__body" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <div class=3D"n=
ote__header" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D"">  <div class=3D"note__title" data=
-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D"">Getting started with an LLM judge</div> </div> <di=
v class=3D"note__content" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <p>If you want to g=
ive it a go, I suggest first reading this <a href=3D"https://huggingface.co=
/learn/cookbook/en/llm_judge" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer"=
>very good guide</a> on how to setup your first LLM as judge!</p><p>You can=
 also try the <a href=3D"https://distilabel.argilla.io/latest/" target=3D"_=
blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">distilabel</a> library, which allows you=
 to generate synthetic data and update it using LLMs. They have a nice <a h=
ref=3D"https://distilabel.argilla.io/latest/sections/pipeline_samples/paper=
s/ultrafeedback/" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">tutorial</a=
> applying the methodology of the <a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.013=
77" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">Ultrafeedback paper</a> a=
s well as a <a href=3D"https://distilabel.argilla.io/latest/sections/pipeli=
ne_samples/examples/benchmarking_with_distilabel/" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D=
"noopener noreferrer">tutorial on benchmarking</a> implementing the Arena H=
ard benchmark.</p> </div> </div> </div>=20
<h4 id=3D"getting-a-judge-model"><a href=3D"https://openevals-evaluation-gu=
idebook.hf.space/#getting-a-judge-model">Getting a Judge-Model</a></h4>
<p>When using an existing LLM, you can go for <a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/=
abs/2306.05685v4" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">generalist,=
 high capability models</a>, <a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.01535" t=
arget=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">small specialist models</a> tr=
ained specifically to discriminate from preference data, or training your o=
wn.</p>
<p><strong>Using a generalist LLM</strong></p>
<p>With the introduction of more capable LLMs (such as ChatGPT), some resea=
rchers started exploring using big models as judges.</p>
<div class=3D"note note--warning" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <!-- When t=
here's a title, emoji is inline with title -->
    <div class=3D"note__body" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <div class=3D"n=
ote__header" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <span class=3D"note__emoji-inlin=
e" aria-hidden=3D"true" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D"">=E2=9A=96=EF=B8=8F</sp=
an> <div class=3D"note__title" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D"">Closed vs open =
source judge models</div> </div> <div class=3D"note__content" data-astro-ci=
d-qg6lmfty=3D""> <p><strong>Closed source models (Claude, GPT-o) tradeoffs:=
</strong></p><p>Disadvantages:</p><ul>
<li><strong>Non-reproducible</strong>: Models can change without notice via=
 API updates</li>
<li><strong>Black box</strong>: Un-interpretable decision-making</li>
<li><strong>Privacy risks</strong>: Data sent to third parties, potential l=
eakage</li>
</ul><p>Advantages:</p><ul>
<li>Easy access without local setup or hardware requirements</li>
</ul><p><strong>Open source models are closing the gap</strong> while solvi=
ng reproducibility and interpretability issues. Models like DeepSeek R1, gp=
t-oss, and the recent Qwen models are now competitive alternatives.</p> </d=
iv> </div> </div>=20
<p>You=E2=80=99ll find a good cost analysis of model providers <a href=3D"h=
ttps://huggingface.co/spaces/ArtificialAnalysis/LLM-Performance-Leaderboard=
" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">here</a> if you need help p=
icking one.</p>
<p><strong>Using a tiny specialized LLM judge model</strong></p>
<p>You can also make the choice to use tiny specialized LLM judges. With of=
ten a couple billion parameters, they can run locally on most recent consum=
er hardware, while being trained from scratch or fine-tuned using instructi=
on data. You often need to follow their specific prompt formats.</p>
<p>Some existing models as of 2024 were Flow-Judge-v0.1 (<a href=3D"https:/=
/huggingface.co/collections/flowaicom/flow-judge-v01-66e6af5fc3b3a128bde07d=
ec" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">weights</a>), 3.8B parame=
ters, a Phi-3.5-mini-instruct fine-tuned on a synthetic preference dataset,=
 Prometheus (<a href=3D"https://huggingface.co/prometheus-eval/prometheus-1=
3b-v1.0" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">weights</a>, <a href=
=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.08491" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener nor=
eferrer">paper</a>), 13B parameters, a model trained from scratch on synthe=
tic preference dataset, and JudgeLM (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.=
17631" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">paper</a>), 7B to 33B =
parameters, models trained from scratch on synthetic preference datasets ge=
nerated with a variety of models. Newer alternatives surely exist!</p>
<p><strong>Training your own</strong>
You can also make the choice to train or fine-tune your own LLM-as-judge. (=
I would avoid doing this, unless you are on a very niche domain).</p>
<p>If you go in that direction, you=E2=80=99ll first need to gather prefere=
nce data for your task of interest, which can come</p>
<ul>
<li>From existing <a href=3D"https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/lmsys-chat=
bot-arena" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">human preference d=
atasets</a></li>
<li>From model generated preference data (which you can generate following =
the above tiny-model judges papers data sections, or get directly, for exam=
ple from the Prometheus <a href=3D"https://huggingface.co/datasets/promethe=
us-eval/Preference-Collection" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer=
">preference</a> and <a href=3D"https://huggingface.co/datasets/prometheus-=
eval/Feedback-Collection" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">fee=
dback</a> collections).</li>
</ul>
<div class=3D"sidenote-wrapper" style=3D"position: relative; display: block=
;"><p>Then you need to decide whether to start from a small model to train =
from scratch, or from an existing model, that you can distill into a new sm=
aller model, or quantize, then fine-tune (using peft or adapter weights if =
the model is big and your training compute low) using the above data.</p><d=
iv class=3D"sidenote-container" style=3D"position: absolute; top: 0px; righ=
t: -292px; width: 260px; display: block; opacity: 1;"> <aside class=3D"side=
note">  Apparently <a href=3D"https://x.com/dk21/status/1826292289930674590=
" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">starting from a reward mode=
l works better than from an instruct model</a>  </aside> </div></div>
 =20
<h4 id=3D"designing-your-evaluation-prompt"><a href=3D"https://openevals-ev=
aluation-guidebook.hf.space/#designing-your-evaluation-prompt">Designing yo=
ur evaluation prompt</a></h4>
<p>Once you=E2=80=99ve selected your model, you need to define what is the =
best possible prompt for your task.</p>
<div class=3D"note note--info" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <!-- When ther=
e's a title, emoji is inline with title -->
    <div class=3D"note__body" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <div class=3D"n=
ote__header" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <span class=3D"note__emoji-inlin=
e" aria-hidden=3D"true" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D"">=F0=9F=93=9D</span> <d=
iv class=3D"note__title" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D"">Prompt design guideli=
nes</div> </div> <div class=3D"note__content" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D"">=
 <p>Provide a clear description of the task at hand:</p><ul>
<li><em>Your task is to do X.</em></li>
<li><em>You will be provided with Y.</em></li>
</ul><p>Provide clear instructions on the evaluation criteria, including a =
detailed scoring system if needed:</p><ul>
<li><em>You should evaluate property Z on a scale of 1 - 5, where 1 means =
=E2=80=A6</em></li>
<li><em>You should evaluate if property Z is present in the sample Y. Prope=
rty Z is present if =E2=80=A6</em></li>
</ul><p>Provide some additional =E2=80=9Creasoning=E2=80=9D evaluation step=
s:</p><ul>
<li><em>To judge this task, you must first make sure to read sample Y caref=
ully to identify =E2=80=A6, then =E2=80=A6</em></li>
</ul><p>Specify the desired output format (adding fields will help consiste=
ncy)</p><ul>
<li><em>Your answer should be provided in JSON, with the following format {=
=E2=80=9CScore=E2=80=9D: Your score, =E2=80=9CReasoning=E2=80=9D: The reaso=
ning which led you to this score}</em></li>
</ul> </div> </div> </div>=20
<p>You can and should take inspiration from <a href=3D"https://github.com/h=
uggingface/lighteval/blob/main/src/lighteval/tasks/extended/mix_eval/judge_=
prompts.pyy" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">MixEval</a> or <=
a href=3D"https://github.com/huggingface/lighteval/blob/main/src/lighteval/=
tasks/extended/mt_bench/judge_prompt_templates.py" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D=
"noopener noreferrer">MTBench</a> prompt templates.</p>
<div class=3D"note note--warning" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <!-- When t=
here's a title, emoji is inline with title -->
    <div class=3D"note__body" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <div class=3D"n=
ote__header" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <span class=3D"note__emoji-inlin=
e" aria-hidden=3D"true" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D"">=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F</sp=
an> <div class=3D"note__title" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D"">To remember whe=
n doing model as judge</div> </div> <div class=3D"note__content" data-astro=
-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <p>Pairwise comparison <a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs=
/2403.16950" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">correlates bette=
r with human preference</a> than scoring, and is more robust generally.</p>=
<p>If you really want a score, use an integer scale make sure you provide a=
 detailed explanation for what <a href=3D"https://x.com/seungonekim/status/=
1749289437165769177" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">each sco=
re represents</a>, or an additive prompt (<em>provide 1 point for this char=
acteristic of the answer, 1 additional point if =E2=80=A6</em> etc)</p><p>U=
sing one prompt per capability to score tends to give better and more robus=
t results</p> </div> </div> </div>=20
<p>You can also improve accuracy using the following, possibly more costly,=
 techniques:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Few shot examples</strong>: like in many other tasks, if you pr=
ovide examples it can help its reasoning. However, this adds to your contex=
t length.</li>
<li><strong>Reference</strong>: you can also enhance your prompt with a ref=
erence if present, which increases accuracy</li>
<li><strong>CoT</strong>: <a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.08073" targ=
et=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">improves accuracy for older gen m=
odels</a>, if you ask the model to output its chain of thought <strong>befo=
re</strong> the score (also observed <a href=3D"https://x.com/seungonekim/s=
tatus/1749289437165769177" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">he=
re</a>)</li>
<li><strong>Multiturn analysis</strong>: can improve <a href=3D"https://arx=
iv.org/abs/2305.13281" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">factua=
l error detection</a></li>
<li>Using <strong>a jury</strong> (many judges, where you pick an aggregate=
 of the answers): <a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.18796" target=3D"_b=
lank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">gives better results</a> than using a sin=
gle model. It can be made considerably less costly by leveraging many small=
er models instead of one big expensive model. You can also experiment with =
using one model with variations on temperature</li>
<li>Surprisingly, the community has found that adding stakes to the prompts=
 (<code>answer correctly and you'll get a kitten</code>) can increase corre=
ctness. Your mileage may vary on this one, adapt to your needs.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you are working on critical tasks (medical domain for example), make =
sure to use methodologies transferred from the humanities, and 1) compute i=
nter-annotator agreement metrics to make sure your evaluators are as unbias=
ed as possible, 2) Use proper survey design methodology when creating your =
scoring grid to mitigate bias. However, most people don=E2=80=99t really wa=
nt a reproducible and high quality unbiased eval, and will be happy with qu=
ick and dirty evaluation through OK-ish prompts. (Which is an OK situation =
to be in! Just depends on the consequences attached).</p>
<h4 id=3D"evaluating-your-evaluator"><a href=3D"https://openevals-evaluatio=
n-guidebook.hf.space/#evaluating-your-evaluator">Evaluating your evaluator<=
/a></h4>
<p>Before using a judge-LLM in production or at scale, you want to evaluate=
 its quality for your task, to make sure its scores are actually relevant a=
nd useful for you.</p>
<div class=3D"note note--neutral" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <!-- When t=
here's no title, emoji is above content -->
    <div class=3D"note__layout" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D"">  <div class=
=3D"note__body" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <div class=3D"note__content" =
data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <p>This will be easier to do if it predicts b=
inary outputs, because you=E2=80=99ll be able to interpretable classificati=
on metrics (accuracy/recall/precision). If it predicts scores on a scale, i=
t will be much harder to estimate the quality of the correlation with a ref=
erence. Models are notoriously bad at predicting on a scale.</p> </div> </d=
iv> </div> </div>=20
<p>So, once you have selected your model judge and its prompt, you=E2=80=99=
ll need to do the following.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Pick your baseline</strong>
You=E2=80=99ll need to compare your evaluator judgments to a baseline: it c=
an be human annotations, the output of another judge model that you know is=
 qualitative on your task, a gold truth, itself with another prompt, etc.</=
li>
</ol>
<div class=3D"note note--info" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <!-- When ther=
e's a title, emoji is inline with title -->
    <div class=3D"note__body" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <div class=3D"n=
ote__header" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <span class=3D"note__emoji-inlin=
e" aria-hidden=3D"true" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D"">=F0=9F=8E=AF</span> <d=
iv class=3D"note__title" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D"">Quality over quantity=
 for baseline</div> </div> <div class=3D"note__content" data-astro-cid-qg6l=
mfty=3D""> <p>You don=E2=80=99t need many baseline examples (50 can suffice=
), but they must be:</p><ul>
<li><strong>Representative</strong>: Cover the full range of your task</li>
<li><strong>Discriminative</strong>: Include edge cases and challenging exa=
mples</li>
<li><strong>High quality</strong>: Use the best reference data you can obta=
in</li>
</ul> </div> </div> </div>=20
<ol start=3D"2">
<li><strong>Pick your metric</strong>
Your metric will be used to compare your judge=E2=80=99s evaluations with y=
our reference.</li>
</ol>
<p>In general, this comparison is considerably easier to do if your model i=
s predicting binary classes or doing pairwise comparison, as you=E2=80=99ll=
 be able to compute accuracy (for pairwise comparison), or precision and re=
call (for binary classes), which are all very easy to interpret metrics.</p=
>
<p>Comparing the correlation of scores with human or model scoring will be =
harder to do. To understand why in more detail, I advise you to read this c=
ool <a href=3D"https://eugeneyan.com/writing/llm-evaluators/#key-considerat=
ions-before-adopting-an-llm-evaluator" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener no=
referrer">blog section on the topic</a>.</p>
<p>In general, if you=E2=80=99re a bit lost about what metrics to pick when=
 (in terms of models, metrics, =E2=80=A6), you can also look at <a href=3D"=
https://eugeneyan.com/assets/llm-eval-tree.jpg" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"no=
opener noreferrer">this interesting graph</a> from <a href=3D"https://eugen=
eyan.com/writing/llm-evaluators/" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener norefer=
rer">the same above blog</a> =E2=AD=90.</p>
<ol start=3D"3">
<li><strong>Evaluate your evaluator</strong>
For this step, you simply need to use your model and its prompt to evaluate=
 your test samples! Then, once you get the evaluations, use your above metr=
ic and reference to compute a score for your evaluations.</li>
</ol>
<p>You need to decide what your threshold for acceptance is. Depending on h=
ow hard your task is, you can aim for 80% to 95% accuracy, if you=E2=80=99r=
e doing pairwise comparison. Regarding correlations (if you=E2=80=99re usin=
g scores), people in the literature tend to seem happy with 0.8 Pearson cor=
relation with a reference. However, I=E2=80=99ve seen some papers declare t=
hat 0.3 indicates a good correlation with human annotators (^^=E2=80=9D) so=
 ymmv.</p>
<h4 id=3D"tips-and-tricks"><a href=3D"https://openevals-evaluation-guideboo=
k.hf.space/#tips-and-tricks">Tips and tricks</a></h4>
<div class=3D"note note--warning" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <!-- When t=
here's a title, emoji is inline with title -->
    <div class=3D"note__body" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <div class=3D"n=
ote__header" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <span class=3D"note__emoji-inlin=
e" aria-hidden=3D"true" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D"">=E2=9A=A0=EF=B8=8F</sp=
an> <div class=3D"note__title" data-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D"">Mitigating well=
 known biases of LLM as judges</div> </div> <div class=3D"note__content" da=
ta-astro-cid-qg6lmfty=3D""> <p>We discussed in this section=E2=80=99s <a hr=
ef=3D"http://localhost:4321/#pros-and-cons-of-using-judge-llms" target=3D"_=
blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">intro</a> a number of LLM judges biases.=
 Let=E2=80=99s see how you should try to mitigate them.</p><p><strong>Lack =
of internal consistency</strong>:
=E2=9E=A1=EF=B8=8F You can mitigate this by doing self-consistency promptin=
g of your judge, prompting it multiple times and keeping the majority outpu=
t</p><p><strong>Self-preference</strong>:
=E2=9E=A1=EF=B8=8F You can mitigate this by using a jury</p><p><strong>Blin=
dness to input perturbation</strong>:
=E2=9E=A1=EF=B8=8F asking the model to explain its reasoning <a href=3D"htt=
ps://twitter.com/seungonekim/status/1749289437165769177" target=3D"_blank" =
rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">before providing a score</a>
=E2=9E=A1=EF=B8=8F or providing a coherent grading scale in the prompt.</p>=
<p><strong>Position-bias</strong>:
=E2=9E=A1=EF=B8=8F switching answer positions randomly
=E2=9E=A1=EF=B8=8F computing the log-probabilities of all possible choices =
to get a normalized answer</p><p><strong>Verbosity-bias</strong> (or length=
-bias):
=E2=9E=A1=EF=B8=8F You can mitigate this by <a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/ab=
s/2404.04475" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">accounting for =
the answer difference in length</a></p><p><strong>Format bias</strong>:
=E2=9E=A1=EF=B8=8F You can mitigate this by paying attention to the trainin=
g prompt format (if the model was instruction tuned) and ensuring you follo=
w it.</p> </div> </div> </div>=20
<p><strong>Picking correct tasks for an LLM judge</strong></p>
<p>LLM evaluators:</p>
<ul>
<li>are <strong>bad at identifying hallucinations</strong> in general, part=
icularly what are called partial hallucinations (which look close to the gr=
ound truth but are actually slightly different) (see <a href=3D"https://arx=
iv.org/abs/2305.11747" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">this</=
a> and <a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.08896" target=3D"_blank" rel=
=3D"noopener noreferrer">this</a>)</li>
<li>have a low to OK-ish correlation with human annotators on <a href=3D"ht=
tps://arxiv.org/abs/2304.02554" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferre=
r">summarization</a> (<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.16634" target=
=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">here too</a>), <a href=3D"https://a=
rxiv.org/abs/2307.16877" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">fait=
hfulness</a>, and are not consistently correlated with human judgement more=
 broadly against <a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.18403" target=3D"_bl=
ank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">a scope of tasks</a></li>
</ul>
<h4 id=3D"what-about-reward-models"><a href=3D"https://openevals-evaluation=
-guidebook.hf.space/#what-about-reward-models">What about Reward Models?</a=
></h4>
<p>Reward models learn to predict a score from human annotations for given =
prompt/completion pairs. The end goal is for them to do predictions aligned=
 with human preference.
Once trained, these models can then be used to improve other models, by act=
ing as a a reward function which is a proxy for human judgment.</p>
<p>The most common type of reward model is the Bradley-Terry model, which o=
utputs a single <strong>pairwise score</strong>, following:</p>
